==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|corruscation||dated}}

===Etymology===
{{bor+|en|la|coruscātiōnem}}, {{m|la|coruscātiō|t=glitter, flash}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌkɒɹəsˈkeɪʃən/}}
* {{rhyme|en|eɪʃən|s=4}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A sudden display of [[brilliance]]; a flashing of light; a [[sparkle]].
#* {{RQ:Carlyle French Revolution|text=[I]n the dusky galleries, duskier with unwashed heads, is a strange ''''coruscation''','—of impromptu billhooks.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1838|author={{w|Letitia Elizabeth Landon}}|title=Duty and Inclination|volume=2|page=85|text=The one, as a brilliant '''coruscation''' playing in a summer sky, might enchant the fancy and ensure the suffrage of a moment; the other, as a lovely constellation, though less vivid, yet from its undeviating steadfastness never failed to leave upon the observer impressions more truly gratifying, solid, and lasting.}}
#* '''2001,''' [[w:Oliver Sacks|Oliver Sacks]], ''[[w:Uncle Tungsten|Uncle Tungsten: Memories of a Chemical Boyhood]]'', [[w:Alfred A. Knopf|Alfred A. Knopf]] (2001), 6,
#*: All of these things—the rubbed amber, the magnets, the crystal radio, the clock dials with their tireless '''coruscations'''—gave me a sense of invisible rays and forces, a sense that beneath the familiar, visible world of colors and appearances there lay a dark, hidden world of mysterious laws and phenomena.

